WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Most Node.js application teams interact with a small set of Kubernetes objects again and again.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.320
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:16.860
It usually contains the Node.js application container and, in some designs, a sidecar for supporting behavior.

4
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:25.280
A Deployment describes the desired state for a replicated stateless workload and manages the creation, replacement, and rollout of Pods.

5
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.540
A Service gives those Pods a stable network identity inside the cluster.

6
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:37.800
Ingress, a gateway, or a load balancer then provides external HTTP routing according to the platform's design.

7
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.280
The relationship between these objects is built on labels and selectors.

8
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.060
The Deployment creates Pods with particular labels.

9
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.600
The Service selects Pods with matching labels.

10
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.500
The routing layer sends traffic to the Service.

11
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.120
If labels are inconsistent, a Service may send traffic to nothing, or to the wrong workload.

12
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:09.060
When a rollout behaves strangely, checking labels, selectors, endpoints, and Pod readiness is often more useful than staring at the application code first.

13
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:16.220
Stateless Node.js APIs are often a natural fit for Deployments because they can run multiple interchangeable replicas.

14
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.960
Stateful data should usually live outside those application Pods or in purpose-built storage systems.

15
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:32.900
A Pod can disappear and be recreated; a Deployment can replace old replicas with new ones; a node can fail and the scheduler can place a replacement elsewhere.

16
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:44.600
Application design should assume that individual Pods are disposable, while persistent state, durable queues, and external databases require their own reliability and security controls.

17
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.480
Readable manifests matter.

18
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:58.580
Teams should be able to look at a Deployment, Service, and Ingress and understand what runs, how traffic reaches it, what labels connect the pieces, which container port is used, and what rollout settings apply.

19
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.520
Kubernetes YAML can become noisy quickly, but the core intent should remain reviewable in pull requests.

20
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:11.820
That reviewability becomes important when debugging traffic, readiness, scaling, and releases under pressure.
